The deque stores indexes whose values are in decreasing order. Before adding a new index, smaller values are removed from the back because the new value is better and will stay in the window longer. The front index then points to the current maximum.

There are two cleanup checks: remove an index that has left from the front, and remove weaker candidates from the back. With `k = 1`, every value becomes its own answer, which the loop handles normally.

```java
class Solution {
    public int[] maxSlidingWindow(int[] arr, int k) {
        if (arr == null || arr.length == 0 || k <= 0 || k > arr.length) {
            return new int[0];
        }

        int[] result = new int[arr.length - k + 1];
        java.util.ArrayDeque<Integer> candidates = new java.util.ArrayDeque<>();
        int resultIndex = 0;

        for (int index = 0; index < arr.length; index++) {
            while (!candidates.isEmpty() && candidates.peekFirst() <= index - k) {
                candidates.pollFirst();
            }

            while (!candidates.isEmpty()
                    && arr[candidates.peekLast()] <= arr[index]) {
                candidates.pollLast();
            }

            candidates.offerLast(index);

            if (index >= k - 1) {
                result[resultIndex++] = arr[candidates.peekFirst()];
            }
        }

        return result;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`. Every index enters and leaves the deque at most once.
Space complexity: `O(k)` for the deque, excluding the returned array. The input is read only.

Kept the direct version here. For each starting position, the code checks all `k` values and stores the largest one. It is easy to follow, but overlapping windows cause many repeated checks.

Dry run for `arr = [1, 3, -1, -3]` and `k = 3`:
- `[1, 3, -1]` gives `3`
- `[3, -1, -3]` gives `3`

The method also returns an empty array for an empty input. It does not change `arr`.

```java
class Solution {
    public int[] maxSlidingWindow(int[] arr, int k) {
        if (arr == null || arr.length == 0 || k <= 0 || k > arr.length) {
            return new int[0];
        }

        int[] result = new int[arr.length - k + 1];

        for (int start = 0; start <= arr.length - k; start++) {
            int currentMaximum = arr[start];

            for (int index = start + 1; index < start + k; index++) {
                currentMaximum = Math.max(currentMaximum, arr[index]);
            }

            result[start] = currentMaximum;
        }

        return result;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O((n - k + 1) * k)`, usually written as `O(nk)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)` besides the returned array.

A multiset keeps the current window sorted and also keeps duplicate values. That makes insertion and removal safe, while the last element is always the maximum.

When a value leaves the window, `find` is used before `erase`. This removes only one copy, which matters when the window contains repeated numbers. For example, with `[4, 4, 2]`, removing one `4` must not remove both.

```cpp
class Solution{
public:
    vector<int> maxSlidingWindow(vector<int> &arr, int k) {
        vector<int> result;

        if (arr.empty() || k <= 0 || k > static_cast<int>(arr.size())) {
            return result;
        }

        multiset<int> window;

        for (int index = 0; index < static_cast<int>(arr.size()); index++) {
            window.insert(arr[index]);

            if (index >= k) {
                auto oldValue = window.find(arr[index - k]);
                if (oldValue != window.end()) {
                    window.erase(oldValue);
                }
            }

            if (index >= k - 1) {
                result.push_back(*window.rbegin());
            }
        }

        return result;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n log k)` because each insert and erase costs `O(log k)`.
Space complexity: `O(k)` for the multiset, not counting the output. The input array is not changed.

## Solution
```java
class Solution {
    public int[] maxSlidingWindow(int[] arr, int k) {
        LinkedList<Integer> stack = new LinkedList<>();
        int[] res = new int[arr.length-k+1];
        for(int i=0; i<arr.length; i++){

            while(!stack.isEmpty() && arr[stack.peek()] < arr[i]){
                stack.pop();
            }
            stack.push(i);
            if(!stack.isEmpty() && i-stack.peekLast()>=k) stack.removeLast();
            if(i>=k-1){
                res[i-k+1] = arr[stack.getLast()];
            }
        }
        return res;
    }
}

```

